  "result": "MobileVLA-R1: Reinforcing Vision-Language-Action for Mobile Robots (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17889v1)\nciteturn6711view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6708view0\",\"lineno\":112}); Total lines: 375\nL112:  | $f_{\\theta}:(X^{\\text{rgb}},X^{\\text{depth}},X^{\\text{point}})\\times I\\rightarrow A,\\quad a_{t}=[V_{x},V_{y},\\omega_{yaw},\\alpha],$  |  | (1)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFT-NCFM: An Influence-Aware Data Distillation Framework for Efficient VLA Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16233v1)\nciteturn6711view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn6708view1\",\"pattern\":\"Fact-Tracing\"}); Total lines: 523\nL52: To this end, this paper introduces FT-NCFM, a fundamentally different, data-centric generative data distillation framework. Our framework employs a self-contained Fact-Tracing (FT) engine that combines causal attribution with programmatic contrastive verification to assess the intrinsic value of samples. Guided by these assessments, an adversarial NCFM process synthesizes a model-agnostic, information-dense, and reusable data asset.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFT-NCFM: An Influence-Aware Data Distillation Framework for Efficient VLA Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16233v1)\nciteturn6711view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn6708view1\",\"pattern\":\"NCFM\"}); Total lines: 523\nL90: Our framework, FT-NCFM, creates an influence-aware NCFM process.\nL228: #### Paradigm Comparison with SOTA Methods\nL229: \nL230: Table 4: Comparison of FT-NCFM with model-centric (policy distillation) and data-centric (coreset selection) methods on the CALVIN benchmark.\nL231: Paradigm  | Method  | Data Ratio (%)  | Total Training Time (GPU-h) $\\downarrow$  | Avg. Len $\\uparrow$\nL232: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL233: Model-Centric  | DROC (ICLR 2023) (cite50†Zha et al. 2024 )  | 100  | 128 + 65 = 193  | 3.05\nL234: Mole-VLA (ICLR 2024) (cite81†Zhang et al. 2025b )  | 100  | 128 + 50 = 178  | 3.20\nL235: RLDG (CoRL 2024) (cite51†Xu et al. 2024 )  | 100  | 128 + 70 = 198  | 3.15\nL236: Data-Centric (Coreset)  | Random Sampling  | 5  | 6.5  | 1.88\nL237: Influence Function Coreset  | 5  | 18  | 2.45\nL238: Data-Centric (Ours)  | FT-NCFM  | 1  | 20.0  | 2.19\nL239: FT-NCFM  | 5  | 25.0  | 3.11\nL240: FT-NCFM  | 10  | 31.5  | 3.47\nL241: The results in Table cite89†4 strongly answer our second research question (Q2). Compared to SOTA policy distillation methods (e.g., RLDG, Mole-VLA), our FT-NCFM, using only 5% of the data and 25 hours of total time, achieves comparable performance (3.11 vs 3.15-3.20) with about one-seventh of their resource consumption. When using 10% of the data, our performance (3.47) far exceeds all policy distillation methods, while the total time (31.5h) is still less than one-sixth of theirs.\nL242: This highlights the fundamental efficiency bottleneck brought by the reliance of policy distillation methods on expensive teacher models. Furthermore, compared to traditional ”selection”-based coreset methods, our ”synthesis”-based FT-NCFM demonstrates enormous superiority.\nL243: ### Cost-Benefit Analysis\nL244: \nL245: A potential concern is the additional overhead introduced by the data preprocessing stage of FT-NCFM. In this section, we quantify this cost using the large-scale LIBERO benchmark as an example.\nL246: \nL247: Table 5: Cost-benefit analysis of each stage of FT-NCFM on the LIBERO dataset.\nL248: Stage  | Description  | Time Cost (GPU-h) for X% Data\nL249: --- | --- | ---\nL250: 1%  | 5%  | 10%\nL251: --- | --- | ---\nL252: SpatialVLA (100% data)  | Train SOTA model on full data  | 192\nL253: --- | --- | ---\nL254: FT-NCFM Framework\nL255: ---\nL256:    FT Engine + NCFM Preprocessing  | One-time investment for value assessment  | 24.0\nL257:    Policy Training on $D_{synth}$  | Policy learning on synthetic data  | 1.5  | 7.0  | 15.0\nL258: FT-NCFM (Total)  | Total Time  | 25.5  | 31.0  | 39.0\nL259: As shown in Table cite90†5 , the preprocessing stage of FT-NCFM (including FT engine assessment and NCFM synthesis) requires about 24 GPU-hours on LIBERO, which is a One-Time Investment. Even when this overhead is included, the total time for FT-NCFM with 10% data (39.0h) is only 20.3% of the standard OpenVLA model training time (192h). This ”invest first, benefit later” model has significant ”amortization benefits” in development cycles that require multiple model iterations.\nL260: ### Ablation Study\nL261: \nL262: To verify the necessity of each component in our FT engine, we conducted a series of ablation experiments.\nL263: \nL264: Table 6: Ablation study of key components of FT-NCFM on the CALVIN benchmark (Avg. Len, @5% data).\nL265: \nL266: Method Variant  | Avg. Len $\\uparrow$\nL267: --- | ---\nL268: FT-NCFM (Full Method)  | 3.11\nL269: Ablating FT Engine Components:  |\nL270:    w/o Contrastive Verification (use $Score_{base}$ only)  | 2.81\nL271:    w/o FT Engine (weighted NCFM with random weights)  | 2.15\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComparative Analysis of Large Language Model Inference Serving Systems: A Performance Study of vLLM and HuggingFace TGI (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17593v1)\nciteturn6711view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn6708view3\",\"pattern\":\"Experimental Setup\"}); Total lines: 573\nL15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:     1. cite7†1.1 Contributions L18:   3. cite8†2 Background and Related Work L19:     1. cite9†2.1 LLM Inference Challenges L20:     2. cite10†2.2 LLM Serving Systems L21:     3. cite11†2.3 Benchmarking Studies L22:   4. cite12†3 Methodology L23:     1. cite13†3.1 Experimental Setup L24:       1. cite14†3.1.1 Hardware Configuration L25:       2. cite15†3.1.2 Software Versions L26:     2. cite16†3.2 Models and Configurations L27:     3. cite17†3.3 Workload Characteristics L28:       1. cite18†3.3.1 Request Patterns L117: ### 2.3 Benchmarking Studies\nL118: \nL119: While several benchmarking efforts exist, they often have limited scope. Prior work has examined individual aspects such as memory efficiency or single-model performance, but comprehensive comparisons across multiple dimensions remain scarce. Our work extends previous efforts by providing systematic evaluation across model sizes, workload patterns, and performance metrics relevant to production deployment decisions.\nL120: \nL121: ## 3 Methodology\nL122: \nL123: ### 3.1 Experimental Setup\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBeyond Surface-Level Similarity: Hierarchical Contamination Detectionfor Synthetic Training Data in Foundation Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17602v1)\nciteturn6711view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn6708view4\",\"pattern\":\"## 3\"}); Total lines: 276\nL68: ## 3 Methodology\nL69: ### 3.1 Problem Formulation\nL70: \nL71: Let $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{syn}}=\\{x_{i}^{s}\\}_{i=1}^{N_{s}}$ denote a synthetic training dataset and $\\mathcal{B}=\\{x_{j}^{b}\\}_{j=1}^{N_{b}}$ a benchmark test set. We define contamination at severity level $\\ell\\in\\{1,2,3,4\\}$ as:\nL96: ### 3.3 Implementation Details\nL97: We implement Level 1 using the official Min-K% Prob codebase with K=20%. For Level 2, we use the all-mpnet-base-v2 sentence transformer, DBSCAN with $\\epsilon=0.15$ and min_samples=5, and multivariate Gaussian distribution fitting. Level 3 extracts CoT traces using regular expressions to parse reasoning steps, computing similarity using weighted Jaccard similarity on n-gram sets.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTensor Gauge Flow Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17616v1)\nciteturn6711view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn6708view5\",\"pattern\":\"Tensor Gauge Fields\"}); Total lines: 466\nL15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Mathematical Background L18:     1. cite8†2.1 Tensor Gauge Fields L19:       1. cite9†2.1.1 Tensor Fields on Fiber Bundles L20:   4. cite10†3 Tensor Gauge Flow Models L21:   5. cite11†4 Experiments L22:     1. cite12†4.1 TGFM Dynamics and Gauge Correction L23:     2. cite13†4.2 Dataset L24:       1. cite14†Component means. L25:       2. cite15†Sampling. L26:     3. cite16†4.3 Training Objective and Optimization L27:     4. cite17†4.4 Results L28:       1. cite18†4.4.1 Training loss L29:       2. cite19†4.4.2 Test loss L41: # Tensor Gauge Flow Models\nL42: \nL43: Alexander Strunk Evercot AI astrunk.research@evercot.ai   Roland Assam Evercot AI rassam.research@evercot.ai ^{†}^{†}thanks: Corresponding author: astrunk.research@evercot.ai\nL44: \nL45: 18 November 2025\nL46: ###### Abstract\nL47: This paper introduces Tensor Gauge Flow Models, a new class of Generative Flow Models that generalize Gauge Flow Models [cite29†23 ] and Higher Gauge Flow Models [cite30†24 ] by incorporating higher-order Tensor Gauge Fields into the Flow Equation. This extension allows the model to encode richer geometric and gauge-theoretic structure in the data, leading to more expressive flow dynamics.\nL48: Experiments on Gaussian mixture models show that Tensor Gauge Flow Models achieve improved generative performance compared to both standard and gauge flow baselines.\nL49: ## 1  Introduction\nL50: \nL51: A Tensor Gauge Flow Model is a generative modeling approach in which the dynamics are governed by the neural ODE:\nL52: \nL53:  | $\\displaystyle\\hat{\\nabla}_{dt}x(t)=\\;v_{\\theta}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\;-\\;\\alpha(x(t),t)\\,\\Pi_{M}\\!\\left(\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\!\\left[\\hat{T}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\right]\\,\\prod_{i=1}^{n}d^{\\mu_{i}}\\!\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\right)$  |\nL54: \nL55: with the following components:\nL56: \nL57:   * •\nL58: $v_{\\theta}(x(t),t)\\in T_{x}M$ is a learnable vector field modeled by a Neural Network.\nL59: \nL60:   * •\nL61: \nL62: $\\alpha(x(t),t)$ is a space and time dependent weight, also modeled by a Neural Network.\nL63: \nL64:   * •\nL65: \nL66: $\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}(x(t),t)$ is the Tensor Gauge Field modeled by a Neural Network.\nL67: \nL68:   * •\nL69: \nL70: $d^{\\mu}(x(t),t)\\in T_{x}M$ is the direction vector field.\nL71: \nL72:   * •\nL73: \nL74: $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$ is a Tensor Field on $C$, which can be learned by a Neural Network.\nL75: \nL76:   * •\nL77: $\\Pi_{M}:C\\rightarrow TM$ is a smooth projection from the vector bundle $C$ to the tangent bundle $TM$ of the base manifold $M$.\nL78: \nL79: The action of the Tensor Gauge Field $\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}(x(t),t)$ can be either:\nL80: \nL81:   * •\nL82: \nL83: Action of a Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{g}$ on the Tensor Field $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$:\nL84:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\!\\left[\\hat{T}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\right]:=\\;\\mathcal{A}^{a}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\,L_{a}\\!\\left[\\hat{T}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\right],$  |\nL85: \nL86: where $\\{L_{a}\\}$ are the generators of $\\mathfrak{g}$ in the representation carried by $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$. The tensor field may be an ordinary vector, $\\hat{T}^{\\alpha}(x(t),t)=\\hat{v}^{\\alpha}(x(t),t)$, or a contraction\nL87:  | $$\\hat{T}^{\\alpha}(x(t),t)=\\sum_{k}T^{\\alpha}{}_{\\nu_{1}\\dots\\nu_{k}}(x(t),t)\\,\\hat{v}^{\\nu_{1}}(x(t),t)\\cdots\\hat{v}^{\\nu_{k}}(x(t),t).$$  |\nL88: \nL89:   * •\nL90: \nL91: Action of an $L_{\\infty}$-algebra on the (graded) Tensor Field $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$:\nL92: \nL93:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\!\\left[\\hat{T}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\right]:=\\;\\sum_{m}\\mathcal{A}^{a}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\,b_{m}\\!\\bigl(e_{a},\\,\\hat{T}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr),\\dots,\\hat{T}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\bigr),$  |\nL94: where $b_{m}$ are the higher multilinear brackets of the $L_{\\infty}$-algebra and $\\{e_{a}\\}$ is a chosen basis. Again, $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$ may be a graded vector $\\hat{v}$ or a graded contracted tensor as above.\nL95: ## 2  Mathematical Background\nL96: \nL97: In this section the mathematical background foundational to Tensor Gauge Flow Models is introduced. The central framework is that of Tensor Fields on Fiber Bundles. Standard references for these Differential-Geometric notions include [cite31†16 , cite32†25 , cite33†1 , cite34†17 , cite35†10 , cite36†11 , cite37†22 , cite38†13 ]. For an intuitive and concise exposition adapted to the present setting, the construction builds on the mathematical concepts described in [cite29†23 , cite30†24 ].\nL98: ### 2.1  Tensor Gauge Fields\nL99: A Tensor Gauge Field is a Tensor Field $\\mathcal{A}^{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{m}}{}_{\\nu_{1}\\dots\\nu_{n}}{}^{a}$ whose coefficients take values in a (graded) vector space carrying either an $L_{\\infty}$-algebra or a Lie-algebra structure. Equivalently, if $E\\to M$ is a (graded) vector bundle with typical fiber $V$, a Tensor Gauge Field on $M$ is a section of $T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes E$. Here, the fiber index ^{a} refers to a choice of local frame of $E$. A Tensor Gauge Flow Model can be visualized locally as follows:\nL100: Figure 1: A Tensor Gauge Flow Model can be visualized locally via its Tensor Gauge Field $\\mathcal{A}^{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{m}}{}_{\\nu_{1}\\dots\\nu_{n}}{}^{a}$. The black region represents the base manifold $M$ on which the trajectory $x(t)$ evolves. Above each point of $M$, the red regions depict the fiber spaces associated with the indices $\\mu_{1},\\dots,\\mu_{m}$ while the green region represents the internal vector space carrying the index $a$.\nL101: The yellow regions illustrate the Tensor Field $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$ evaluated along the flow, which is acted on by $\\mathcal{A}$ to produce the Tensor Gauge correction in the dynamics.\nL102: #### 2.1.1  Tensor Fields on Fiber Bundles\nL103: \nL104: In mathematical terms, an $(m,n)$-Tensor Field $\\hat{T}^{(m,n)}$ on a manifold $M$ is a smooth section:\nL105: \nL106:  | $$\\hat{T}^{(m,n)}\\in\\bigl\\{\\,S:M\\rightarrow T^{(m,n)}M\\ \\big|\\ \\pi\\circ S=\\mathrm{Id}_{M}\\,\\bigr\\},$$  |\nL107: \nL108: where the $(m,n)$-Tensor bundle is\nL109: \nL110:  | $$T^{(m,n)}M:=\\bigsqcup_{x\\in M}T^{(m,n)}_{x}M,$$  |\nL111: \nL112: with projection $\\pi:T^{(m,n)}M\\rightarrow M$, and fibers\nL113: \nL114:  | $$T^{(m,n)}_{x}M:=(T_{x}M)^{\\otimes m}\\otimes(T^{*}_{x}M)^{\\otimes n}.$$  |\nL115: In local coordinates $x^{\\mu}$, a Tensor Field has the form\nL116: \nL117:  | $$\\hat{T}^{(m,n)}=\\hat{T}^{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{m}}{}_{\\nu_{1}\\dots\\nu_{n}}\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x^{\\mu_{1}}}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x^{\\mu_{m}}}\\otimes dx^{\\nu_{1}}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes dx^{\\nu_{n}}.$$  |\nL118: A (smooth) Fiber Bundle is a surjective map $\\pi:E\\rightarrow M$ with typical fiber $F$ such that for every $x\\in M$ there is an open neighborhood $U$ and a diffeomorphism $\\phi_{U}:\\pi^{-1}(U)\\!\\to U\\times F$ satisfying $\\mathrm{pr}_{1}\\circ\\phi_{U}=\\pi$ (local triviality).\nL119: There are now two possibilities to express a Tensor Gauge Field on a Fiber Bundle $E\\rightarrow M$:\nL120: \nL121:   1. 1.\nL122: \nL123: Tensor Field on the base manifold valued in the fiber:\nL124:  | $$\\hat{T}^{(m,n)}\\;\\in\\;\\bigl\\{\\,S:M\\rightarrow T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes E\\ \\big|\\ \\pi_{T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes E}\\circ S=\\mathrm{Id}_{M}\\,\\bigr\\},$$  |\nL125: \nL126: valued in the typical fiber $F$ (assuming $E$ is a vector bundle, e.g., an associated bundle).\nL127: \nL128:   2. 2.\nL129: \nL130: Tensor Field on the total space:\nL131: \nL132:  | $$\\hat{T}^{(m,n)}\\;\\in\\;\\bigl\\{\\,S:E\\rightarrow T^{(m,n)}E\\ \\big|\\ \\pi_{T^{(m,n)}E}\\circ S=\\mathrm{Id}_{E}\\,\\bigr\\}.$$  |\nL133: ## 3  Tensor Gauge Flow Models\nL134: \nL135: A Tensor Gauge Flow Model is defined on a (graded) Fiber Product Bundle $\\hat{A}$:\nL136: \nL137:  | $$\\hat{A}=B\\times_{M}C$$  |\nL138: \nL139: where $B\\to M$ and $C\\to M$ are smooth Fiber Bundles over the same base:\nL140: \nL141: The dynamics of the Tensor Gauge Flow Model is governed by the following ODE:\nL142:  | $\\displaystyle\\hat{\\nabla}_{dt}x(t)=\\;v_{\\theta}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\;-\\;\\alpha(x(t),t)\\,\\Pi_{M}\\!\\left(\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\!\\left[\\hat{T}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\right]\\,\\prod_{i=1}^{n}d^{\\mu_{i}}\\!\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\right)$  |\nL143: \nL144: with the following ingredients:\nL145: \nL146:   * •\nL147: \nL148: $v_{\\theta}(x(t),t)\\in TM$ represents a learnable vector field modeled by a Neural Network.\nL149: \nL150:   * •\nL151: \nL152: $\\alpha(x(t),t)$ is a space-and time-dependent scalar weight, also modeled by a Neural Network.\nL153: \nL154:   * •\nL155: $\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}(x(t),t)$ is the Tensor Gauge Field modeled by a Neural Network.\nL156: \nL157:   * •\nL158: \nL159: $d^{\\mu}(x(t),t)\\in TM$ is the direction vector field.\nL160: \nL161:   * •\nL162: \nL163: $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$ is a Tensor Field on $C$, which can be parameterized by a Neural Network.\nL164: \nL165:   * •\nL166: \nL167: $\\Pi_{M}:C\\rightarrow TM$ is a smooth bundle map projection from the vector bundle $C$ to the tangent bundle $TM$ of the base manifold $M$.\nL168: The Tensor Gauge Field $\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}(x(t),t)$ is encoded by the Fiber Bundle $B$. The fibers of $B$ are different for the following two cases:\nL169: \nL170:   * •\nL171: \nL172: $L_{\\infty}$-algebra-valued case: The fibers of $B$ carry a graded vector space $\\hat{B}$ underlying an L_{∞}-algebra, and $\\mathcal{A}$ is a smooth section of the tensor bundle $T^{(m,n)}M$ with values in $\\hat{B}$:\nL342: For the gauge-based models this loss is augmented with two regularizers acting on the Lie-algebra-valued Tensor Gauge Field:\nL343: \nL344:  | $$\\mathcal{L}(\\theta)=\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{FM}}(\\theta)+\\lambda_{A}\\,\\mathcal{L}_{A}(\\theta)+\\lambda_{\\text{cons}}\\,\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{cons}}(\\theta),$$  |\nL345: \nL346: where:\nL347: \nL348:   * •\nL349: \nL350: $\\mathcal{L}_{A}(\\theta)$ penalizes large Gauge Fields, implemented as the mean squared Frobenius norm of the $\\mathfrak{so}(N)$-matrix section:\nL382: ### 4.4  Results\nL383: \nL384: Across all considered dimensions $N$, the Tensor Gauge Flow Models consistently outperform both the plain Flow Models and the standard Gauge Flow Models.\nL385: #### 4.4.1  Training loss\nL386: \nL387: Figure cite41†2 compares the training losses of all model variants. The Tensor Gauge Flow Models achieve the lowest training loss across all dimensions $N$.\nL388: \nL389: cite42†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Training loss comparison (lower is better). The training loss for each model is normalized by the loss of the PlainVF + Tensor Gauge Field model. Values are shown for several ambient dimensions $N$.\nL390: #### 4.4.2  Test loss\nL391: \nL392: Figure cite43†3 shows the corresponding test losses. The Tensor Gauge Flow Models again outperform all other model variants across all dimensions $N$, indicating improved generalization rather than mere overfitting.\nL393: \nL394: cite44†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: Test loss comparison (lower is better). The test loss for each model is normalized by the loss of the PlainVF + Tensor Gauge Field model. Values are shown for several ambient dimensions $N$.\nL395: #### 4.4.3  Number of parameters\nL396: \nL397: As shown in Figure cite45†4 , the Plain Flow Model uses a slightly larger number of parameters than the Tensor Gauge Flow Models across all dimensions $N$.\nL398: \nL399: cite46†Image: Refer to caption Figure 4: Number of trainable parameters for each model family as a function of ambient dimension $N$.\nL400: ## 5  Related Work\nL401: \nL402: The concept of Tensor Gauge Flow Models simply generalizes both the framework of Gauge Flow Models [cite29†23 ] and Higher Gauge Flow Models [cite30†24 ] from a $1$-form Gauge Field to higher-order Tensor Gauge Fields. There are several lines of work that are closely related to this construction, spanning gauge-based Flow Models, Riemannian and geometric Flow Matching, and recent high-order Flow Matching formulations.\nL406: Tensor Gauge Flow Models can be viewed as a further generalization of GFM and HGFM in which the gauge correction term is constructed from higher-rank Tensor Fields (rather than only $1$-forms) valued in a Lie or $L_{\\infty}$-algebra (or its module), thereby enriching the geometric structure available to the model.\nL412: Tensor Gauge Flow Models adopt the same FM/RFM training paradigm but modify the model vector field by Tensor-valued gauge corrections defined on Fiber Bundles over the data manifold.\nL416: These works demonstrate how FM-style objectives can be lifted from first-order dynamics to higher-order trajectories; Tensor Gauge Flow Models are complementary in that they lift the state-dependent correction to higher-order Tensor Gauge Fields on bundles, rather than augmenting the temporal order of the ODE alone.\nL418: Another direction develops Flow Matching on “higher-order” geometric spaces such as spaces of distributions or structured manifolds. Wasserstein Flow Matching (WFM) lifts FM to the Wasserstein space of probability measures, allowing generative modeling over families of distributions (e.g., shapes or cell populations) by learning flows in distribution space rather than sample space [cite54†12 ].\nL419: Metric Flow Matching introduces a metric-aware version of Conditional Flow Matching that interpolates along geodesics in data-dependent metrics, further emphasizing the role of underlying geometry in FM objectives [cite55†15 ]. These approaches can be interpreted as Flow Matching on higher-level geometric objects (spaces of measures, metric manifolds), while Tensor Gauge Flow Models focus on enriching the local geometric structure through Tensor-valued Gauge Fields and higher algebraic data.\nL420: ##### Gauge- and Geometry-aware Neural Architectures.\nL421: Finally, Tensor Gauge Flow Models are related in spirit to gauge-equivariant and geometry-aware neural architectures. Gauge-equivariant convolutional networks generalize group-equivariant CNNs to local gauge transformations on manifolds, using gauge connections and differential forms to define consistent convolutions on curved domains and spherical meshes [cite56†8 , cite57†14 ].\nL422: Riemannian and geometric deep learning methods more broadly exploit manifold structure, vector fields, and differential forms to define intrinsic operations on curved spaces [cite58†21 , cite59†3 ].\nL423: Unlike these architectures, which encode gauge or geometric structure at the level of layer design, Gauge Flow Models, Higher Gauge Flow Models, and Tensor Gauge Flow Models integrate gauge-theoretic structure directly into the continuous-time generative dynamics via gauge-corrected vector fields trained with Flow Matching or Riemannian Flow Matching.\nL424: In summary, Tensor Gauge Flow Models extend the Gauge Flow and Higher Gauge Flow frameworks to higher-order Tensor Gauge Fields, while remaining compatible with the Flow Matching and Riemannian Flow Matching objectives that underpin many recent geometric and high-order generative models.\nL425: ## References\nL426:   * [1] M. Berger and B. Gostiaux (1988) Differential geometry: manifolds, curves, and surfaces. Graduate Texts in Mathematics, Springer New York. External Links: ISBN 9783540966265, LCCN 87027507, cite60†Link†books.google.de Cited by: cite61†§2 .\nL427:   * [2] M. Braun, N. Jaquier, L. Rozo, and T. Asfour (2024) Riemannian flow matching policy for robot motion learning. In 2024 IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems (IROS), External Links: cite62†Document†dx.doi.org Cited by: cite63†§5 .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBehind the Screens: Uncovering Bias in AI-Driven Video Interview Assessments Using Counterfactuals (https://arxiv.org/html/2505.12114v2)\nciteturn6711view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn6708view6\",\"pattern\":\"Methodology\"}); Total lines: 541\nNo matching text found for \"Methodology\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAeroVerse: UAV-Agent Benchmark Suite for Simulating, Pre-training, Finetuning, and Evaluating Aerospace Embodied World Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2408.15511v2)\nciteturn6711view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn6708view2\",\"pattern\":\"Evaluation Metrics\"}); Total lines: 580\nL30:     8. cite20†V-H Image Enhancement L31:   7. cite21†VI Experiments L32:     1. cite22†VI-A Baselines L33:     2. cite23†VI-B Evaluation Metrics L34:   8. cite24†VII SkyAgentX: unifying perception, reasoning, planning, and navigating L35:     1. cite25†VII-A Aerospace Embodied Chain-of-thought L36:     2. cite26†VII-B Aerospace Embodied Multitask Curriculum Learning L37:     3. cite27†VII-C SkyAgent & SkyAgentX L38:   9. cite28†VIII Results L39:     1. cite29†VIII-A Quantitative Analysis L40:     2. cite30†VIII-B Qualitative Analysis L41:   10. cite31†IX Discussion L67: cite51†Image: Refer to caption Fig. 1: The benchmark suite for the aerospace embodiment world model, AeroVerse, comprises one simulation platform (AeroSimulator), two real-virtual pre-training datasets (AerialAgent-Ego15k and CyberAgent-Ego500k), five downstream task instruction datasets (SkyAgent-Scene3k, SkyAgent-Reason3k, SkyAgent-Nav3k and SkyAgent-Plan3k, and SkyAgent-Act3k ), and more than ten evaluation metrics (SkyAgent-Eval).\nL102: Based on the high-quality datasets and evaluation metrics, we propose SkyAgentX, the first UAV-agent embodied large model integrating “perception-reasoning-navigating-planning”. Through the innovative introduction of the aerospace embodied chain-of-thought mechanism, SkyAgentX achieves closed-loop collaboration of environmental perception, spatial relationship reasoning, and multi-step task planning.\nL112: (6) We design over $10$ 2D$\\//$3D visual-language models, $2$ pre-training datasets, $5$ downstream task instruction datasets, and $10+$ evaluation metrics, as well as a simulator featuring $4$ urban scenarios, into a benchmark suite, AeroVerse, which will be released to the community to advance the field of aerospace embodied agents.\nL294: ### VI-B Evaluation Metrics\nL295: \nL296: Traditional Metrics. Common indicators include BLEU-1, BLEU-2, BLEU-3, and BLEU-4[cite93†37 ]. Compare the degree of overlap between the n-grams in the candidate translation and the reference translation. It is commonly used for evaluating translation quality and can be divided into multiple evaluation indicators based on n-grams.\nL449: Results on SkyAgent-Nav3k. Similarly to the evaluation metric with SkyAgent-Reason3k, we employ LLM-JUDGE-NAV to assess human preferences. In this task, GPT-4o demonstrates particularly strong performance, ranking at the top across most urban scenarios and evaluation metrics, including vocabulary level, semantic level, and human preference.\n"
}
